---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Layout title="params.require vs params[]: what's the failure mode?" description="Practice card. Two ways to read a nested key, two different errors when it's missing. Drills strong parameters.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Reading the Source · Card 3</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4"><code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-3xl">require</code> vs <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-3xl">[]</code>: what's the failure mode?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">Two ways to reach into params for the same data. When the form submits without the expected key, they fail differently. The difference is more than a stack trace.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The code
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">Two strong-params idioms. A user submits the form without the <code>post</code> key. What does each line do?</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`def post_params
  params.require(:post).permit(:title, :body)
end

# vs

def post_params
  params[:post].permit(:title, :body)
end`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A POST comes in without a <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">post</code> key in the body. <strong>What exception does each version raise, and what's the right Rails error message for a missing form key?</strong></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <div id="prompt-section" class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-7">
            <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Take a moment.</strong> Both fail. The errors are different. Which one tells you something useful about what's missing?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pt-2">
            <button
              id="show-solution-btn"
              class="w-full px-6 py-3 bg-ruby text-white rounded-lg hover:bg-ruby-dark transition-colors font-medium"
            >
              Reveal the answer →
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="solution-section" class="hidden space-y-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-green-600">✅</span> The answer
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require</code> raises a clear, specific error. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">[]</code> raises a vague one.</strong></p>
            <div class="border-2 border-green-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-green-50/30">
              <CodeBlock code={`# Version 1: params.require(:post).permit(...)
# → raises ActionController::ParameterMissing
#   "param is missing or the value is empty: post"
#
# Rails renders this as a 400 Bad Request automatically.

# Version 2: params[:post].permit(...)
# → params[:post] is nil
# → nil.permit(...) raises NoMethodError
#   "undefined method 'permit' for nil:NilClass"
#
# Rails renders this as a 500 Internal Server Error.`} language="ruby" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>💡</span> Why this matters
            </h3>
            <div class="space-y-3 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              <p><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require</code> is doing exactly one thing: assert the key exists, and tell you which key in the error message. A missing form key is a 400 (the client sent bad input), not a 500 (your code crashed).</p>
              <p>The <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">[]</code> version makes the same request look like a server bug. Your error tracker pages you on it. The fix is the same in both cases, but only one of the two errors tells you what to fix.</p>
              <p>The Rails idiom is <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require</code> for a reason. Use it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>📚</span> Theory
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For the full walkthrough of how <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">params</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">permit</code> work, read <a href="/lessons/source-params" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark font-medium">Card 5 of Reading the Source</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-when-sql-runs" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 2</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-before-action-halt" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 4 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <script>
    const btn = document.getElementById('show-solution-btn');
    const prompt = document.getElementById('prompt-section');
    const solution = document.getElementById('solution-section');
    if (btn && prompt && solution) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        prompt.classList.add('hidden');
        solution.classList.remove('hidden');
        if (window.innerWidth < 1024) {
          solution.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
        }
      });
    }
  </script>
</Layout>
